# Documentation and decisions

- Separate stakeholder requirements, accepted engineering decisions, assumptions, and unresolved questions.
- Do not retroactively rewrite accepted ADR history. Supersede it with a new ADR.
- Every non-trivial feature has a spec with: problem, scope, non-goals, actors/permissions, behavior, state/data changes, errors, security/privacy, observability, migrations, tests, rollout, rollback, and acceptance criteria.
- Use concrete dates and versions in operational records. Avoid “latest” without a checked date.
- Update the roadmap and backlog when scope or sequencing changes.
- Update diagrams when state transitions, boundaries, or payment/moderation flow changes.
- Document commands that were actually verified. Mark examples clearly when not run.
- Completion notes list files changed, migrations, tests/commands run and results, operational changes, unresolved issues, and rollback considerations.
- Keep policy decisions in ADRs/product docs, not only in code comments or chat history.
